<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramakrishna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1836">1836</a>–<a href="/years/1886">1886</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ramakrishna Paramahamsa, Ramakrishna Chattopadhyay, Gadadhar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ramakrishna was an Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> mystic, born in Kamarpukur in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> Presidency, who became a priest at the Dakshineshwar Kali Temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> at around the age of twenty. A devotee of the goddess Kali, he also followed practices of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a>, Tantric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaktism">Shaktism</a> and Advaita <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a>, as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and taught through parables that the world's religions are paths to one goal. In 1859 he married Sarada Devi, and in 1865 the monk Tota Puri initiated him into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sannyasa">sannyasa</a>. He founded the monastic Ramakrishna Order and died of throat cancer in 1886; his followers regard him as an avatar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramakrishna.jpg" alt="Ramakrishna" width="330" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abinash Chandra Dna · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramakrishna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ramakrishna was born on 18 February 1836 in Kamarpukur, in the Hooghly district, into a poor and pious Bengali Brahmin family, the fourth and youngest child of Khudiram Chattopadhyaya and Chandramani Devi. At nine he was invested with the sacred thread, and he later described his first experience of samadhi at ten or eleven, while crossing a paddy field. He learned to read and write at the village school but lost interest in what he called bread-winning education. He became skilled at making images, acting and painting, and at fourteen left school to start a drama group.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Dakshineswar Kali Temple, founded by the widow Rani Rasmani after a dream, was dedicated on 31 May 1855 by Ramakrishna's brother Ramkumar. At about twenty Ramakrishna became absorbed in the worship of Kali, singing songs by Ramprasad. Rumours that he had become unstable led his mother and brother to arrange his marriage in 1859 to Saradamani Mukhopadhyaya, then five years old, later known as Sarada Devi. Over the following years he practised many paths, including devotion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, the Vaishnava attitudes of a mother to the child Rama and of the gopis to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, and Tantra.</p><p>At the end of 1864 the wandering monk Tota Puri arrived at Dakshineswar, and in 1865 initiated him into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sannyasa">sannyasa</a>. In 1866 Govinda Roy, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> who had adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> practice, initiated him into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and Ramakrishna reasoned that this too was a path to God.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1875 Ramakrishna met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmo-samaj">Brahmo Samaj</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keshab-chandra-sen">Keshab Chandra Sen</a>, who established the New Dispensation on principles drawn from Ramakrishna and publicised his teachings. Most of his prominent disciples came between 1879 and 1885, among them Narendranath Dutta, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swami-vivekananda">Swami Vivekananda</a>. His teachings were recorded by Mahendranath Gupta in the Sri Sri Ramakrishna Kathamrita, published in five volumes from 1902 to 1932.</p><p>At the beginning of 1885 he developed a throat ailment that became throat cancer, and in December he was moved to a garden house at Cossipore, where monastic disciples and Sarada Devi nursed him. He died in August 1886. Vivekananda founded the Ramakrishna Math and Mission in 1897. Scholars such as Narasingha Sil have argued that Vivekananda recast Ramakrishna as an Advaita Vedantin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ramakrishna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In modern times <b>Ramakrishna</b>, Vivekananda and Mohandas Gandhi gave Hindu ideas new forms and a world audience.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li></ul></section>
